surface-current-rule-3fc0544f.symmetry_origin:
This rule is a direct consequence of Ampere's Law in its integral form, \( \nabla \times \boldsymbol{H} = \boldsymbol{J} \) (or \( \nabla \times \boldsymbol{B} = \frac{1}{
u_0}\boldsymbol{J} \) in vacuum where \( \boldsymbol{H} = \frac{1}{
u_0}\boldsymbol{B} \)), when applied to an Amperian loop that straddles a surface carrying a current density. By considering a small rectangular loop with one side of infinitesimal length parallel to the surface and the other sides perpendicular, and letting the perpendicular sides shrink to zero, the tangential component of the magnetic field's curl is directly related to the surface current.
